# Governed Lookups and Computed Action Eligibility

A lookup field should use the target domain's approved projection and filters.
An action checkbox should reflect the row's current eligibility. This workbook
executes both patterns with PostgreSQL, using session-local temporary tables.
It needs an existing database through `SELECTO_LIVEBOOKS_DB*`, with no migrations
or sample-app seeds.

## Setup

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_components!()
```

## 1) Compute eligibility in the root query

The shared domain declares `eligible` as a boolean predicate over `state` and
`ready`. There is no physical `eligible` database column. The SQL expression
evaluates it with the row, avoiding a separate policy request for each checkbox.
`internal: true` is a UI hint; it does not enforce access control.

```elixir
import ExUnit.Assertions
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/action_fixture.exs", __DIR__))
connection = SelectoLivebooks.ActionFixture.connect!()
source = SelectoLivebooks.ActionFixture.domain()
runtime = Selecto.Runtime.Context.new(SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter, connection)

queue =
  Selecto.configure(source, runtime)
  |> Selecto.with_tenant(tenant_id: 101)
  |> Selecto.apply_tenant_scope()
  |> Selecto.select(["id", "eligible"])
  |> Selecto.order_by({"id", :asc})

{sql, params} = Selecto.to_sql(queue)
refute sql =~ "selecto_root.eligible"
assert "open" in params
assert true in params
assert {:ok, {rows, _, _}} = Selecto.execute(queue)
assert rows == [[1, true], [2, false], [4, true], [5, nil]]

records = Map.new(rows, fn [id, eligible] ->
  {to_string(id), %{field_values: %{"eligible" => eligible}}}
end)
assert SelectoComponents.Actions.eligible_selected_ids(
  source.actions.approve, ["1", "2", "3", "4", "5", "999"], records) == ["1", "4"]
%{sql: sql, rows: rows}
```

Unknown eligibility (`NULL`) and missing records do not enable selection.
The predicate grammar here is `[:and, [[:eq, field, value], ...]]`; action
preconditions have their own filter grammar. At apply time the host still
enforces capability, tenant, transition and readiness guards, as demonstrated
in [guarded actions](selecto_action_execution_workbook.livemd).

## 2) Give the target domain its own lookup view

These carriers are synthetic. Two tenants have a carrier beginning with
“Alpine,” and one carrier in tenant 101 is inactive. The query-library segment
and required tenant filter must exclude those unsuitable matches.

```elixir
Postgrex.query!(connection, """
CREATE TEMP TABLE livebook_carriers (
  id bigint PRIMARY KEY, tenant_id bigint NOT NULL, name text NOT NULL,
  city text NOT NULL, active boolean NOT NULL
)
""", [])
for row <- [
  [10, 101, "Alpine Freight", "Denver", true],
  [11, 101, "Alpine Retired", "Boulder", false],
  [12, 202, "Alpine Other Tenant", "Denver", true],
  [13, 101, "Coastal Transport", "Seattle", true]
] do
  Postgrex.query!(connection, "INSERT INTO livebook_carriers VALUES ($1,$2,$3,$4,$5)", row)
end

defmodule CarrierLookupLibrary do
  use Selecto.QueryLibrary.DSL
  defsegment active_carriers do
    where(:active, eq: true)
  end
  defprojection label do
    fields([:id, :name, :city])
  end
  defordering name_order do
    order_by(:name, :asc)
    order_by(:id, :asc)
  end
  defview carrier_lookup do
    segment(:active_carriers)
    projection(:label)
    ordering(:name_order)
  end
end

carrier_domain = %{
  name: "Carrier lookup", tenant_required: true,
  source: %{
    source_table: "livebook_carriers", primary_key: :id,
    fields: [:id, :tenant_id, :name, :city, :active],
    columns: %{
      id: %{type: :integer}, tenant_id: %{type: :integer},
      name: %{type: :string}, city: %{type: :string, label: "City"},
      active: %{type: :boolean}
    }, associations: %{}
  },
  schemas: %{}, joins: %{}, query_library: CarrierLookupLibrary.query_library()
}
target =
  Selecto.configure(carrier_domain, runtime, mode: :strict)
  |> Selecto.with_tenant(tenant_id: 101)
  |> Selecto.apply_tenant_scope()
```

## 3) Declare and execute the source's co-domain lookup

The source names a target domain and a view. The host resolves that declaration
to the configured target and supplies its trusted scope. The declaration does
not open a connection or authenticate a user. In this notebook the target is
explicit; a host with several targets should resolve through an allowlist.

```elixir
source = Map.put(source, :co_domains, %{
  carriers: %{
    domain: :carrier, view: :carrier_lookup,
    search: %{fields: [:name, :city], mode: :prefix, configuration: "simple", rank: true},
    result: %{value_field: :id, label_field: :name, description_fields: [:city]}
  }
})
assert {:ok, normalized, diagnostics} = Selecto.Domain.validate(source)
assert :co_domains in diagnostics.canonical_sections
assert Selecto.Domain.project(normalized, :ui).co_domains == source.co_domains

lookup_plan = Selecto.CoDomain.plan(source, target, :carriers, "Alp", limit: 5)
{lookup_sql, lookup_params} = Selecto.to_sql(lookup_plan.query)
assert "alp:*" in lookup_params
assert 101 in lookup_params
assert true in lookup_params
refute lookup_sql =~ "alp:*"

assert {:ok, %{results: results}} =
  Selecto.CoDomain.lookup(source, target, :carriers, "Alp", limit: 5)
assert [%{value: "10", label: "Alpine Freight", description: "City Denver"}] = results
results
```

Return `results` to the client. The returned `query` contains host-side metadata.
Search text is bound; rank ordering comes before the named target ordering.
Keep pagination limits and endpoint authorization in the host.

## 4) Reject malformed or broadened requests

Prefix punctuation with no tokens returns an empty result. A caller-supplied
scope is conjoined with the target's required scope; a conflicting tenant cannot
replace it. Projection mistakes fail during planning.

```elixir
assert {:ok, %{results: []}} = Selecto.CoDomain.lookup(source, target, :carriers, "!!!")
assert {:ok, %{results: []}} = Selecto.CoDomain.lookup(source, target, :carriers, "Alp",
  scope: {"tenant_id", 202})
assert_raise ArgumentError, fn -> Selecto.CoDomain.plan(source, target, :carriers, "") end
assert_raise ArgumentError, fn -> Selecto.CoDomain.plan(source, target, :carriers, "Alp", limit: 101) end
assert_raise ArgumentError, fn -> Selecto.CoDomain.plan(source, target, :missing, "Alp") end

bad_projection = put_in(source, [:co_domains, :carriers, :result, :label_field], :active)
assert_raise ArgumentError, ~r/absent from its projection/, fn ->
  Selecto.CoDomain.plan(bad_projection, target, :carriers, "Alp")
end
```

A chosen lookup value is presentation data. Revalidate membership, tenant and
current business rules when saving an action input. The local LiveView frontend
does not yet implement every grouped/co-domain action-input control; executing
this lookup does not demonstrate such a control.

## 5) Close the isolated session

```elixir
:ok = GenServer.stop(connection)
```

Both temporary tables disappear. To rerun, begin at the fixture cell with a new
connection. For validation of dependency and consumer declarations, continue
with [domain contracts](selecto_domain_contracts_workbook.livemd).
